From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bobsixsixsix-boop <304558835+bobsixsixsix-boop@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 05:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1044] arm64: errata: work around the Qualcomm Oryon DC ZVA
 erratum: trap and emulate it at EL0, never execute it in the kernel

Why
---
Snapdragon X Elite machines (Qualcomm Oryon, MIDR part 0x001) hang and then
hard-reset the whole SoC when a DC ZVA (zero one DCZID_EL0.BS block by VA)
is executed around a CPU cluster entering its power-collapse idle state
(hamoa "cluster_cl5"). The public evidence, in order of strength:

 - icecream95/x1e-crash (crash.c, 2026-05..08): a single-threaded, unpinned
   user-space loop "dc civac two lines -> dc zva one line -> dependent
   read-back through the other line, usleep(10 ms) every 60 rounds so the
   cluster reaches CL5" resets four X1E/X1P laptops within seconds to
   minutes. Its README: removing SCTLR_EL1_DZE from INIT_SCTLR_EL1_MMU_ON
   is a mitigation; Windows keeps DZE on and is not affected (its
   mitigation is unknown).
 - Jens Glathe, "[PATCH RFC] arm64: dts: qcom: hamoa: Drop cluster_cl5
   idle state from CPU clusters", 2026-06-04, linux-arm-msm
   (20260604-dc_zva_mitigation, read through the ratatoskr.run mirror):
   "The cluster_cl5 idle state triggers DC ZVA misbehavior that resets X1
   SoCs"; dropping CL5 made a T14s G6, a ThinkBook 16 G7 and two IdeaPads
   stable; the Dev Kit (platform-coordinated firmware, no OSI) still
   crashes and needs cpuidle.off=1; "a proper fix would be a Qualcomm
   firmware update or a kernel erratum that disables DZE on these SoCs".
 - Marc Zyngier's out-of-tree erratum (arm-platforms.git commit
   7d6ed4b78a0c: clear SCTLR_EL1.DZE, emulate the trapped EL0 DC ZVA) and
   justririll/x1e-sleep kernel/experimental/x1-dc-zva-erratum-and-cl5.patch,
   which carries that erratum as WORKAROUND_QCOM_ORYON_DC_ZVA, extends it
   to the kernel's own DC ZVA users (clear_page, memset, MTE) and turns CL5
   back on for all three hamoa clusters. git.kernel.org sits behind an
   anti-bot page for us, so Marc's original was not read; the x1e-sleep
   copy was, and this patch follows it.
 - Mainline v7.3-rc4/rc5 still has CL5 in hamoa.dtsi and no DC ZVA
   workaround in cpu_errata.c (checked against the raw v7.3-rc4 and
   current master cpu_errata.c/cputype.h/cpucaps).

This tablet (Lenovo TB323FU, Y700 Gen5, SM8850 "kaanapali") has the same
Oryon family, one generation on: MIDR 0x512f0021 = implementer 0x51, part
0x002, r2p1 (dmesg "Booting Linux on physical CPU 0x0000000000
[0x512f0021]"), which upstream cputype.h does not name yet. Its cluster
state "cluster_cl5" 0x01000054 (entry 2150 us, exit 1983 us, min residency
9144 us) is refused in runtime idle by patch 0118 (allow_cluster_off=0)
because the upstream author saw silent resets "line up with cluster0 being
turned off and on". linux/power/cx-collapse/RESEARCH.md 2.4 ranks the
DC ZVA x CL5 interaction as hypothesis H2: the only hypothesis with an
independent public reproducer, but with two hard counter-arguments
(Android runs cl5 on the same firmware while bionic's memset uses DC ZVA;
kaanapali's cl5 is named "ret-pll-off", which may keep L2, unlike X1's CL5
that flushes it). PROTOCOL.md E2 therefore first runs the x1e-crash
sequence with allow_cluster_off=1; this patch is the kernel for the
decisive A/B after a positive E2 (y11: with it, allow_cluster_off=1 must
stop resetting; with qcom_oryon_dc_zva=off it must reset again). It is a
local, unverified port: no X1E reproducer has been run here and nothing in
it has been compiled.

What it changes (all on top of v7.3-rc4; none of these files is touched by
tb323fu-linux 0001-0130 except arch/arm64/Kconfig, where 0021/0022 add
ARM64_BUILTIN_FDT/BOOT_MARKS at the end of "Kernel Features", far from the
errata menu edited here; drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-psci-domain.c is not
touched, so 0106/0118/1043 are unaffected)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
 - arch/arm64/include/asm/cputype.h: QCOM_CPU_PART_ORYON_V3 0x002 and
   MIDR_QCOM_ORYON_V3. The name is ours (Qualcomm calls this core 3rd gen
   Oryon); whether the Snapdragon 8 Elite's Oryon v2 shares part 0x002 is
   not known, the comment says so.
 - arch/arm64/tools/cpucaps: WORKAROUND_QCOM_ORYON_DC_ZVA (same name as
   x1e-sleep/Marc), sorted after WORKAROUND_QCOM_ORYON_CNTVOFF.
 - arch/arm64/Kconfig: QCOM_ORYON_ERRATUM_DC_ZVA, default y, in the
   "ARM errata workarounds via the alternatives framework" menu next to
   the Falkor entries. Documentation/arch/arm64/silicon-errata.rst gets
   the row.
 - arch/arm64/kernel/cpu_errata.c: a LOCAL_CPU_ERRATUM entry "Qualcomm
   Oryon DC ZVA erratum" matching MIDR_QCOM_ORYON_X1 and _V3 (all
   variants). Its cpu_enable() clears SCTLR_EL1.DZE (sysreg_clear_set, no
   explicit isb: the eret back to EL0 is the context sync, as the sibling
   UCI/UCT cache-trap errata rely on) on every CPU: for the boot CPU in setup_system_capabilities(),
   for the others through stop_machine() there or
   verify_local_cpu_capabilities() when they come up later, including
   after hotplug. cpu_do_resume() restores the saved SCTLR_EL1 and our
   idle states keep EL1 context in firmware, so the bit stays clear
   across idle and s2idle. early_param "qcom_oryon_dc_zva": "off" makes
   matches() return false, so the capability is never set: DZE stays 1,
   no alternative is patched, no debugfs file appears, one pr_warn says
   the workaround was disabled. parse_early_param() runs in setup_arch(),
   before smp_prepare_boot_cpu() detects the boot CPU's errata.
 - arch/arm64/include/asm/esr.h + arch/arm64/kernel/traps.c: with DZE=0
   an EL0 DC ZVA traps with EC 0x18 and op0=1 op1=3 op2=1 crn=7 crm=4 (QEMU
   target/arm/helper.c aa64_zva_access/DC_ZVA agree), which the existing
   EL0_CACHE_OP hook already matches (its mask ignores CRm). Add
   ESR_ELx_SYS64_ISS_CRM_DC_ZVA = 4 and a case in
   user_cache_maint_handler(): emulate_dc_zva() reads DCZID_EL0.BS, aligns
   the untagged address down to the block and clear_user()s it. Faults
   behave like the store they are: SIGSEGV through arm64_notify_segfault()
   with the original (tagged) address, exactly like the other emulated
   cache ops (a read-only page gives SEGV_ACCERR; an unmapped hole gets
   SEGV_MAPERR only when it lies above the last VMA, because that helper
   uses find_vma() -- pre-existing upstream behaviour). A per-boot
   counter of emulated instructions is exported as
   /sys/kernel/debug/qcom_oryon_dc_zva_emulated (0444, created by a
   late_initcall only when the capability is on, so the file doubles as
   the "workaround active" indicator).
 - arch/arm64/lib/clear_page.S, memset.S, mte.S: the kernel runs at EL1,
   where DZE does not apply and DCZID_EL0 keeps reading DZP=0, so the
   kernel's own fast paths would keep executing DC ZVA / DC GZVA. Each
   "tbnz dczid, #4, no_zva" test gets an alternative_if
   ARM64_WORKAROUND_QCOM_ORYON_DC_ZVA / b no_zva in front of it (a NOP
   until the capability is set at setup_system_capabilities();
   alternatives relocate branches that leave the block). x1e-sleep does
   the same with an unconditional branch; keying it on the capability
   keeps other CPUs, and the =off boot, on the original code. On this
   tablet FEAT_MOPS is not detected (no "Memory Copy and Memory Set
   instructions" in dmesg), so __pi_memset_generic and the DC ZVA loop of
   __pi_clear_page are the live paths; the MOPS SET* paths are untouched.
   Before alternatives are applied the kernel still executes DC ZVA
   (early memset/clear_page): harmless, the cluster states are not
   reachable before cpuidle-psci registers (OSI firmware, the OS requests
   them). KVM nVHE is not built (VIRTUALIZATION off), KASAN_HW_TAGS is off
   (mte-kasan.h's dc gva/gzva are not compiled), MTE is not detected on
   this SoC (mte.S is patched for completeness).

What user space sees
--------------------
DCZID_EL0.DZP reads as 1 at EL0 while SCTLR_EL1.DZE is 0: that is
architectural (the kernel cannot and need not trap the DCZID_EL0 read), so
glibc, bionic, musl-free Go, OpenJDK and FEX check the bit and fall back
to store loops without ever trapping. Code that ignores DZP (hand-written
JITs) traps once per block, about a microsecond each, and shows up in the
debugfs counter; the result is still correct. DC GVA/GZVA from EL0 (MTE,
not present here) also trap but are not emulated: they reach
do_el0_undef() -> SIGILL, which is what a DZP-ignoring MTE program
deserves. Tag-checked user memory with a non-zero tag would make the
emulation (an untagged kernel store) fault where the hardware instruction
might not; irrelevant without MTE.

Cost
----
Not measured (nothing built here). Expected: EL0 none for DZP-aware
libraries; kernel clear_page()/memset() lose the ZVA fast path and zero
with stnp/stp pairs (clear_page: 4 x stnp per 64 bytes); page-fault-heavy
workloads may lose a few percent. The y11 verify script prints the
measured trap cost (ns per emulated DC ZVA) from the EL0 self-test.

How to switch
-------------
 - Build time: CONFIG_QCOM_ORYON_ERRATUM_DC_ZVA (kbuild-y11.sh forces =y
   and asserts it).
 - Boot time: qcom_oryon_dc_zva=off on the kernel command line disables
   the workaround on affected CPUs (no other value is recognised). Our
   kernels use CONFIG_CMDLINE_FORCE, so kbuild-y11.sh also builds the twin
   image Image-t49-y11-dczva-off (same kernel, same modules, only that
   parameter appended) for the A/B.
 - No run-time switch: SCTLR_EL1 could be re-set on all CPUs but patched
   alternatives cannot be undone, so none is offered.

How to verify it booted
-----------------------
  dmesg | grep 'CPU features: detected: Qualcomm Oryon DC ZVA erratum'
  ls -l /sys/kernel/debug/qcom_oryon_dc_zva_emulated      (0444, a count)
  dczva-selftest (linux/kernel-y8/dczva-selftest.c, EL0, no root needed
  except for the counter): DCZID_EL0.DZP=1; a dc zva on a 0xA5-filled page
  leaves exactly one zeroed block (also through a tagged pointer); dc zva
  on a read-only page -> SIGSEGV/SEGV_ACCERR, on an unmapped page ->
  SIGSEGV; the debugfs counter grows by the number of dc zva executed; it
  prints ns per emulated instruction.
  With qcom_oryon_dc_zva=off: dmesg has "Qualcomm Oryon DC ZVA workaround
  disabled on the command line" and no "detected:" line, the debugfs file
  is absent, the self-test (--expect-dze) sees DZP=0 and native speed.
  y11-verify.sh runs all of this; y11-selfcheck.sh greps the source tree
  and the patch file for every hunk before the build.

Unverified / honest limits
--------------------------
 - Not compiled, not booted, no X1E reproducer run; the port was checked
   hunk by hunk against the raw v7.3-rc4 files and applies cleanly to
   them (plus 0021/0022 for Kconfig) with git apply --check.
 - Whether kaanapali's cl5 ("ret-pll-off") shares X1's mechanism is
   unknown; the patch is the test article, not a conclusion.
 - Whether FEAT_MOPS SET* instructions (absent here) would be affected is
   unknown.
 - The part-0x002 naming (ORYON_V3) and the X1 entry in the MIDR list
   follow x1e-sleep; Qualcomm has published neither an erratum number nor
   a statement.

